\title{An Equivalence Has an Inverse}
\taxon{Proposition}
\import{macros}
\p{
    For every map #{f:A\to B}, an [equivalence](def-005C) determines a
    [map with an inverse](def-005I). Explicitly, there is a canonical function
    ##{
        \mathsf{isEquiv}(f)\to\mathsf{hasInverse}(f).
    }
}
\proof{
    Suppose that #{f} is equipped with a section and a retraction,
    ##{
        (g,G):\mathsf{sec}(f),
        \qquad
        (h,H):\mathsf{retr}(f).
    }
    Thus #{g,h:B\to A},
    #{G:f\circ g\sim\mathsf{id}_B}, and
    #{H:h\circ f\sim\mathsf{id}_A}. First construct a homotopy
    #{K:g\sim h}. For each #{y:B}, define
    ##{
        K(y)
        \defeq
        H(g(y))^{-1}\cdot\mathsf{ap}_h(G(y))
        :g(y)=h(y).
    }
    Indeed, the two factors have types
    ##{
        H(g(y))^{-1}:g(y)=h(f(g(y))),
        \qquad
        \mathsf{ap}_h(G(y)):h(f(g(y)))=h(y),
    }
    so their concatenation is well typed.

    We now make the section #{g} into a left inverse as well. For each #{x:A},
    define
    ##{
        H_g(x)
        \defeq
        K(f(x))\cdot H(x)
        :g(f(x))=x.
    }
    Therefore #{H_g:g\circ f\sim\mathsf{id}_A}, while the original homotopy
    #{G} already gives #{f\circ g\sim\mathsf{id}_B}. Hence
    ##{
        (g,G,H_g):\mathsf{hasInverse}(f).
    }
}
